      "title": "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "908d5028-7875-5863-8cd1-762c56db6dd5",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\right.\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
